window.docContents[134].resolve({"/Basic-Types/Strings/#string-runtime":{"contents":"Memory layout of stringsopen Illuminate in\nopen Manual.Diagram in\nlayoutDiagram [\n  (\"m_header\", .header, txt \"Lean object header\"),\n  (\"m_size\", .size_t, twoLine \"Byte count\" \"size_t\"),\n  (\"m_capacity\", .size_t, twoLine \"Allocated space\" \"size_t\"),\n  (\"m_length\", .size_t, twoLine \"Characters\" \"size_t\"),\n  (\"m_data\", .data none,\n    some <| .styledText (base := fieldLabelStyle) <|\n      \"String data\\n\" ++ family \"monospace\" \"char\" ++ \" array\"),\n  (\"'\\\\0'\", .data (some 30), none)\n]\n\n\nStrings are represented as dynamic arrays of bytes, encoded in UTF-8.\nAfter the object header, a string contains:\n\n byte count\n\nThe number of bytes that currently contain valid string data\n\n capacity\n\nThe number of bytes presently allocated for the string\n\n length\n\nThe length of the encoded string, which may be shorter than the byte count due to UTF-8 multi-byte characters\n\n data\n\nThe actual character data in the string, null-terminated\n\n\n\nMany string functions in the Lean runtime check whether they have exclusive access to their argument by consulting the reference count in the object header.\nIf they do, and the string's capacity is sufficient, then the existing string can be mutated rather than allocating fresh memory.\nOtherwise, a new string must be allocated.\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009Basic Types\u0009Strings","header":"20.8.2. Run-Time Representation","id":"/Basic-Types/Strings/#string-runtime"},"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Managing-Toolchains-with-Elan/#elan-run":{"contents":"The commands in this section provide the ability to run a command in a specific toolchain and to locate a tool from a particular toolchain on disk.\nThis can be useful when experimenting with different Lean versions, for cross-version testing, and for integrating Elan with other tools.\n\n\n\nConfigures an environment to use the given toolchain and then runs the specified program.\nThe toolchain will be installed if the --install flag is provided.\nThe command may be any program; it does not need to be a command that's part of a toolchain such as lean or lake.\nThis can be used for testing arbitrary toolchains without setting an override.\n\n\n\nDisplays the full path to the toolchain-specific binary for command.\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009Build Tools\u0009Elan\u0009Command-Line Interface","header":"24.2.3.5. Running Tools and Commands","id":"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Managing-Toolchains-with-Elan/#elan-run"},"/Introduction/#technical-terms":{"contents":"技术术语是指在撰写技术资料时以非常特定的含义使用的术语，例如本参考资料中的术语。\n技术术语通常会通过类似这样的链接超链接到其定义页面。\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009简介\u0009排版方式","header":"1.2.3. 技术术语","id":"/Introduction/#technical-terms"},"/Iterators/Iterator-Definitions/#Lean-__________________--Iterators--Iterator-Definitions--Loops":{"contents":"IteratorLoop α m provides efficient implementations of loop-based consumers for α-based\niterators. The basis is a ForIn-style loop construct.Its behavior for well-founded loops is fully characterized by the LawfulIteratorLoop type class.This class is experimental and users of the iterator API should not explicitly depend on it.\nThey can, however, assume that consumers that require an instance will work for all iterators\nprovided by the standard library.Iteration over the iterator it in the manner expected by for loops.\n\nThis is the default implementation of the IteratorLoop class.\nIt simply iterates through the iterator using IterM.step. For certain iterators, more efficient\nimplementations are possible and should be used instead.\n\nAsserts that a given IteratorLoop instance is equal to IteratorLoop.defaultImplementation.\n(Even though equal, the given instance might be vastly more efficient.)The implementation of IteratorLoop.forIn in i is equal to the default implementation.\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009Iterators\u0009Iterator Definitions","header":"22.2.4. Loops","id":"/Iterators/Iterator-Definitions/#Lean-__________________--Iterators--Iterator-Definitions--Loops"}});